# Who must approve a change to this repository, and why.
#
# This repository publishes what OSS Verify asserts: the theorem statements, the binary each statement is about, and
# the table that says which statements are proved.  One rule decides every line below.
#
#   A person approves a change to what we ASSERT, or to what DECIDES whether an assertion is checked.
#   A machine catches everything else, and reads every line rather than a path name.
#
# The owners are @sjawhar and @JasonGross, the two people who own /theories/ in the internal repository.  The ruleset
# of main requires a code owner's approval and no other approval, so a change that touches none of the paths below
# needs nobody, and a change that touches one of them needs one of those two.
#
# WHAT WE ASSERT.  Owned.
#
#   Statements.v          The theorems themselves, one file per fuzz target.  Nothing else here is the claim.
#   /theories/            Driver.v, Images.v, Premises.v, Shapes.v: the definitions every Statements.v is written
#                         against.  A change here changes what every statement means without touching a statement.
#   harness.yaml          harness.yaml pins by SHA-256 the ELF file each statement is about; project.yaml names the
#   project.yaml          library and the commit it was built from; release.yaml pins the semantics, the Rocq version
#   /release.yaml         and the checker and runner images.  A swapped binary makes a true statement false without
#                         touching the statement, so the file that chooses the binary is owned exactly as the
#                         statement is.
#   /projects/*/*/source/ The harness source we publish as the thing that was built.
#   /index/               The published tables: statements.tsv, proofs.tsv, binaries.tsv, harnesses.tsv, projects.tsv
#                         and their two rendered pages.  `oft generate` writes them and the lint job holds them equal
#                         to their rendering, so they cannot drift on their own; they are owned because the row that
#                         says open or proved is the claim a reader reads.  This costs a prover nothing: the six proof
#                         pull requests open when this file was written (#19 to #24) change 412, 282 and similar
#                         numbers of files between them and not one file under index/, because the tables are
#                         rendered again when a release is cut.
#
# WHAT DECIDES WHETHER CHECKING HAPPENS.  Owned.
#
#   /.github/             The workflows, the actions they call and actionlint's configuration.
#   /tools/, /oft,        The tools those workflows run: `oft lint`, `oft generate --check`, `oft check`.  ./theorem
#   /theorem              is a one-line shell script that executes ./oft.
#   /toolchain/           allowed-axioms.txt, which decides which axioms a proof may use, and the checker and runner
#                         images' Dockerfiles.  A proof is only as good as this list.
#   /tests/               The positive controls that must pass and the negative controls that must be refused
#                         (tests/check.sh, tests/negative/, tests/mutate.py).  A change here can leave every other
#                         check passing over nothing.
#   /.claude/, AGENTS*.md What an agent loads in a job that holds a token.  CLAUDE*.md and /.mcp.json match no file
#   CLAUDE*.md,           in this repository today; they are listed so that adding one later does not open a hole
#   /.mcp.json            that nobody notices.
#   /.gitattributes       A path marked -diff is not shown in review.
#   /.github/CODEOWNERS   This file, which says who owns the rest.
#
# WHAT THE MACHINE OWNS, AND NOBODY SIGNS.  Deliberately absent from the list below.
#
#   Proof bodies -- everything under projects/<project>/<target>/proofs/ -- have no owner.  The Rocq kernel is their
#   reviewer: a proof checks against the statement or it does not, and a human reading adds nothing to that verdict.
#   Sami ruled this on 2026-11-07: "It is correct that the proof body needs no Human Reviewer"
#   (dispatch://OSSV-286/comment/c7500b0c-e76c-4cb0-bf86-9dea75718d29).
#
#   The same holds for everything else a machine can check line by line, and for the same reason -- a path name is a
#   worse reader than the check that reads the content:
#     README.md at every level, Images.v, Distribution.v, the dune files and dune-project are written by
#       `oft generate` from the owned facts, and the lint job's `oft generate --check` fails if they differ;
#     projects/<project>/<target>/counterexamples/ is replayed against the pinned binary;
#     projects/<project>/<target>/proofs/provenance/ is regenerated by made_from.py --check;
#     Characterise.v states in its own first lines that "These are not claims": it poses a problem whose answer is
#       a program plus a proof, and the proof is checked by the kernel like any other.
#   docs/ is prose about the claims and is not the claim; theories/ and Statements.v are.
#
# The last pattern that matches a file decides its owner, so these lines stay the last ones in this file.
